India's space story began in a fishing village. In the early 1960s, the scientist [[@vikram-sarabhai|Vikram Sarabhai]] chose [[@space-rocket-bicycle|Thumba]], near Thiruvananthapuram in [[@kerala|Kerala]], as a launch site. It lies close to the magnetic equator, a good place to study the upper atmosphere.

{{story:vikram-sarabhai}}

A local church, St Mary Magdalene, was handed over for the project, and the bishop's house became an office. Rocket parts were carried to the pad on a bicycle and a bullock cart. On 21 November 1963, a small American-made Nike-Apache rocket rose from Thumba. Among the young engineers on the team was A. P. J. [[@apj-kalam|Abdul Kalam]], who later led India's own rocket programme.

Passing the egg is the most dangerous moment. The egg, about as heavy as three cricket balls, must roll from her feet to his without touching the ice for long. In such cold, an egg left uncovered for a minute or two can freeze, killing the chick inside.

Some couples fumble it, and the egg is lost. Once the egg is safe, the female leaves on a trek that can be tens of kilometres to the open sea.

{{story:antarctica}}

Weaving complex patterns used to mean a helper sitting on top of the loom, lifting threads by hand. In 1804, Joseph-Marie Jacquard in France fitted looms with chains of punched cards. A hole meant "lift this thread". No hole meant "leave it down". The loom could weave a detailed picture by reading the cards.

The English inventor [[@charles-babbage|Charles Babbage]] planned to use punched cards to give instructions to his calculating engine. A hole or no hole is a yes-or-no choice, the same idea as the [[@binary-code|0s and 1s]] inside computers today.

{{story:charles-babbage}}

Under the strings of an electric guitar sit pickups: small magnets wrapped in thousands of turns of very fine copper wire. When a steel string vibrates above them, it disturbs the magnetic field. That creates a tiny, fast-changing electric current in the wire, matching the string's movement exactly.

The signal travels down a cable to an amplifier, which makes it powerful enough to drive a loudspeaker. Guitarists can also pass the signal through effects boxes to make it echo, buzz or wail.

{{story:magnets-how}}

Hand spinning had faded so much that when Gandhi wanted to start, he could not find a charkha or anyone to teach him. In his autobiography he tells how a friend, Gangaben Majmudar, searched for one. In 1917 she finally found spinning wheels in Vijapur, a village in Gujarat, stored away in people's lofts.

Soon spinning was being taught at Gandhi's ashram in [[@ahmedabad|Ahmedabad]]. Gandhi himself spun every day, even in prison and on long journeys.

{{story:salt-march}}

The first Peace Prize, in 1901, went to Henry Dunant, who founded [[@red-cross|the Red Cross]], and Frédéric Passy, a French campaigner for peace. Since then, winners have included presidents, doctors, [[@jobs-journalist-day|journalists]] and teachers.

Two Indian citizens have won it: [[@mother-teresa|Mother Teresa]] in 1979, for her work with the poor in Kolkata, and [[@kailash-satyarthi|Kailash Satyarthi]] in 2014, for freeing children from forced work. He shared his prize with Malala Yousafzai of Pakistan, the youngest winner ever. American winners include [[@martin-luther-king|Martin Luther King Jr]], who won in 1964 at the age of 35.

{{story:martin-luther-king}}

A proposed law is called a [[bill|bill]]. A minister or member introduces it in one house. Members debate it, sometimes send it to a small committee for a closer look, and then vote. The same thing happens in the other house. When both houses agree, the bill goes to the President for assent, and it becomes an Act.

The rules for all of this are set out in the [[@constitution|Constitution]], which Parliament can change only with special, larger majorities. B. R. Ambedkar, who chaired the committee that drafted it, wanted the rules hard to bend.

{{story:ambedkar}}

When 400 million tonnes of people come down at once, they hit the ground with about as much energy as a few hundred tonnes of TNT explosive. That sounds alarming. But it would be spread across a huge crowd, and the soil and rock would soak much of it up.

Instruments called seismometers, which measure shaking, would record it clearly, probably across the world. People nearby would feel a thud. Scientists expect it would be more like a small [[@earthquakes|earthquake]] than a disaster.

{{story:earthquakes}}

Shapes that cover a surface without gaps or overlaps form a tessellation. Islamic artists became masters of this, covering walls with stars and polygons that repeat in complex ways. The [[@spain|Alhambra palace in Spain]] is one of the most famous examples.

The Dutch artist M. C. Escher visited the [[@alhambra|Alhambra]] and was inspired. He went on to make [[@tessellations|tessellations]] of lizards, birds and fish that lock together like jigsaw pieces. He also drew [[@puzzle-impossible-shapes|impossible buildings]], such as staircases that seem to go up forever in a loop.

{{story:taj-mahal}}

When India had only one or two TV channels, a weekly cartoon was an event. In the early 1990s, Doordarshan showed The Jungle Book, an anime series made in Japan and based on Rudyard Kipling's stories set in Indian forests.

{{story:jungle-book}}

Its Hindi theme song, "Jungle Jungle Baat Chali Hai", had words by the poet Gulzar and music by Vishal Bhardwaj. Many adults can still sing it. Disney's DuckTales, with Scrooge McDuck and his nephews, and TaleSpin, with Baloo as a pilot, were also dubbed into Hindi with catchy theme songs.

For most of history, bright colours were hard to make. Red came from insects or roots, and blue came from indigo plants, much of it grown in India. Purple was the rarest of all. In the ancient Mediterranean it came from sea snails, and thousands were needed to dye one robe.

Then, in 1856, [[@accident-mauve|William Perkin]], an 18-year-old student in London, was trying to make quinine, a malaria medicine, from coal tar. Instead, he got a purple goo. He realised it could dye silk, and named it mauve. Within a few years, mauve dresses were everywhere, and chemists rushed to invent new colours.

{{story:colour-meaning}}

Landing is the hardest part. A gymnast falling from a high flip can hit the mat with a force several times their body weight. By bending the knees and hips, they spread that impact over a slightly longer time, which reduces the strain on [[@bones|bones]] and joints.

Judges reward a landing with no steps, called sticking it. Floors and mats help. The floor exercise area is 12 metres square and sits on springs or foam blocks, giving gymnasts extra bounce.

{{story:bones}}

Older books said that movies work because each picture "persists" on the eye for a moment. Scientists now think that is not the real reason. Your [[@eyes-see|eyes]] send signals to your brain, and special parts of the brain detect motion by comparing what changes from moment to moment. Show the brain pictures that change smoothly, and it reports movement.

There is a second trick. At 24 frames a second, the screen would flicker. So [[@projectors|projectors]] flash each frame two or three times before moving on. Your eyes see 48 or 72 flashes a second, too fast to notice the flicker.

{{story:brain-works}}

Only two short written accounts describe the event. One mentions birds, which might have included wild turkey or ducks, and five deer brought by the Wampanoag. There was probably corn, and perhaps fish and shellfish from the coast.

There was almost certainly no pumpkin pie, because the settlers had no ovens or flour for pastry. Potatoes were not yet common in New England. Much of the menu Americans eat today came later, during the 1800s. Turkey became the centre of the meal partly because it was large enough to feed a whole family and was raised on many American farms. Cranberries, which grow in bogs in New England, became the standard sauce.

{{story:farming-began}}

In the late 1990s and 2000s, cyber cafés sprang up in towns all over India. For a small hourly fee, people could check email, chat with friends, apply for jobs or look up exam results. Many Indians got their first email address in a café.

In 1998, the government opened internet service to private companies. Prices fell and speeds slowly rose. Mobile data was a different story. Before 2016, a gigabyte often cost more than ₹200, so most people used it sparingly. Broadband, a faster always-on connection, spread in cities in the 2000s, often through cable TV operators.

{{story:undersea-internet}}

Gutenberg's key idea was a mould for casting letters quickly from a mix of lead, tin and another metal called antimony. Every "a" came out the same size and shape. Workers called compositors picked letters from cases and lined them up backwards, so they would print the right way round.

The famous Gutenberg Bible was finished around 1455. About 180 copies were printed, some on paper and some on animal skin called vellum. Fewer than 50 survive today, and one of them is at the Library of Congress in Washington, D.C.

{{story:paper-invention}}

The grass decides everything. Wildebeest follow the rains, chasing fresh green shoots in a loop of hundreds of kilometres through Tanzania and Kenya. More than a million of them make the trip, along with hundreds of thousands of zebras and gazelles.

The most dramatic moment is the crossing of the Mara River, usually between July and October. Thousands of animals bunch up on the bank, then plunge in. [[@croc-ancient|Crocodiles]] wait in the brown water. Lions and hyenas wait on the far side. Most wildebeest make it across, and the ones that don't feed the hunters for weeks.

{{story:migration}}

Sangam poems are short and full of nature. Poets sorted their world into five landscapes: mountains, forests, farmland, seashore and dry wasteland. Each landscape had its own flowers, animals, gods and feelings. A poem set in the mountains was about lovers meeting. One set by the seashore was about waiting and longing.

Another group of poems, called *puram*, praised kings, battles and generosity. These verses give historians a picture of trade, food and daily life in early south India. Later came the [[@thirukkural|Thirukkural]], 1,330 short couplets of advice on life, still quoted in Tamil homes and speeches and painted inside government buses.

{{story:cholas}}

Kneading isn't just mixing. Every push and fold lines up the gluten proteins into long, stretchy strands. You can test it: a well-kneaded dough can be pulled so thin you can almost see light through it.

Wheat has lots of gluten, which is why wheat makes tall loaves. Rye, millet and rice have little or none. Breads made from them are flatter or need other tricks. Indian rotis need no yeast at all. They puff up on a hot tawa because water inside turns to steam.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}

The vanilla orchid grows wild in the forests of Mexico. There, the Totonac people grew it long ago, and the Aztecs flavoured their chocolate drink with it. A local bee and perhaps hummingbirds pollinate it naturally.

When Europeans took vanilla vines to other tropical places, the vines grew happily but almost never made pods. The right bees weren't there. For about 300 years, Mexico was nearly the only place that could produce vanilla.

{{story:pollinators}}

[[@northeast-food|Northeast India]] has some of the country's richest insect dishes. In Assam, the pupae of eri silkworms, left over after silk is made, are fried and eaten. In parts of Nagaland and Manipur, people cook hornet and wasp larvae, water beetles and crickets. Insects are often sold in local markets alongside vegetables and fish.

For many families these foods are seasonal treats, gathered at the right time of year.

{{story:ants-world}}
